I finally ascended an illiterate caveman on my 8th attempt.  I was 
mainly trying to convince myself I could do without Elbereth.  That 
wasn't too hard, and the other difficulties of illiteracy also weren't 
so bad---I managed a -30 AC and a +5 Excalibur (see below).  I even got 
a genocide (MMF) out of a throne.

Three potentially interesting techniques I wanted to share:

(1) I collected *every* last weapon generated before the castle, and 
price-ided to find the enchantment.  I found some reasonably good things 
(a +3 elven spear was my main weapon until the castle), but the real 
find was a stack of +5 darts.  These I blessed, then polypiled until I 
got a long sword; I wished for a helm of opposite alignment and got 
the +5 Excalibur.  I was lucky to get +5, but pretty much every game 
should allow at least +3, which is still a pretty good weapon.

(2) Wonderfully fun technique for any game: an ice box and cockatrice 
corpses.  Number the corpses so they don't stack.  Carry them around and 
use them for major battles (throne rooms, etc.).  To avoid being 
stressed, I crossed every level thrice (drop my bag, cross with the 
ice box, drop the ice box, go back for the bag, cross with the bag).
Tedious, but a great way to handle big battles, and you get good potion 
loot.

(3) I managed to get through Gehennom in just a couple of hours.  I dug 
down until I found Asmodeus, who isn't scary at all if you have cold 
resistance; then I dug down to the bottom level with him, then used him 
to find all the upstairs all the way back up.  The only levels I needed 
to map myself were the wizard's tower levels, which have undiggable 
floors. 

